-- title:
--   Linear syzygies among the variables are skew-symmetric
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring, let $m$ be a natural number, and let $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to B[X_0,\dots,X_{m-1}]$ be a family of polynomials in $m$ variables over $B$ indexed by the same finite index set as the variables, satisfying $\sum_{j} c_j X_j = 0$. The assertion is that there exists a family $e : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to B[X_0,\dots,X_{m-1}]$, that is, an $m \times m$ matrix of polynomials, with three properties: $e_{jk} = -e_{kj}$ for all $j,k$; $e_{jj} = 0$ for all $j$; and $c_j = \sum_{k} e_{jk} X_k$ for every $j$. Thus every linear relation among the variables of a polynomial ring over a commutative ring is obtained from the trivial relations $X_j X_k - X_k X_j = 0$ by a skew-symmetric matrix of coefficients, the vanishing of the diagonal being asserted separately from skew-symmetry (the two are not equivalent over a ring in which $2$ is not regular).
--
--   This is the vanishing of $H_1$ of the Koszul complex of the sequence $X_0,\dots,X_{m-1}$ over a commutative ring, in the explicit form that first syzygies of a regular sequence are generated by the Koszul ones. It is used by [`MvPolynomial.relation_mem_span_of_forall_isHomogeneous_mem_span`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.relation_mem_span_of_forall_isHomogeneous_mem_span) in the analysis of relations between homogeneous components.
